    "units": "millions",
    "notes": "MODEL: owner-FCF DCF (dcf-methods routing: cyclical operating company, section 7 adaptations) with three depleting-reserve adaptations, because 2P reserves have halved in four years (283 -> 156 MMboe) and the 2P reserve life is 8.0 years: (1) a 10-year explicit path that rises with FY27 guidance (full year of Waitsia) then DECLINES as reserves deplete - trailing CAGRs are not used as drivers; (2) terminal value capped at a low FCF multiple tied to remaining reserve life (base 7x, bull 9x, bear 5x) with zero/negative terminal growth, so the cap binds in every scenario and the sensitivity grid is flat across terminal growth by design; (3) all-in capex incl. abandonment held at 28-33% of revenue rather than pure sustaining, so restoration and reserve replacement are paid for in the flows. Not a reserve-based NAV: Beach discloses no PV-10 or per-asset reserve economics, and its 65-year record of replacing reserves via exploration and M&A argues against a pure run-off. The bear case IS the run-off. Interest income (8.0) stripped for consistency with the EBITDA-based component build even though the company is net-debt. Cash tax modelled at 25% (statutory 30%; 5yr average cash rate ~19% because exploration and development are deductible; FY26's 179.7 included ~66 of FY25 catch-up). Working-capital capture 0%. FY0 revenue is total revenue (1,921.2) so the 54.1% underlying EBITDA margin is on that base (57.7% on sales revenue)."
